"""Report fixed-geometry detector/copy grid and all failed settings."""
import json,sys
from pathlib import Path
from docx import Document
from docx.oxml import OxmlElement
from docx.text.paragraph import Paragraph
ROOT=Path(__file__).resolve().parents[1];sys.path.insert(0,str(ROOT/'experiments'));import paper50 as P
p=ROOT/'paper/MEGA27-23b-50p.docx';d=Document(p);title='Matched geometry and copy budgets: stronger detection does not settle the frontier'
assert not any(x.text==title for x in d.paragraphs)
j=json.loads((ROOT/'results/block_matched_geometry_audit.json').read_text())
P.h1(d,title)
P.para(d,'The unequal-length whole-file pilot leaves detector cost entangled with noise exposure. We therefore froze a new local comparison plan before opening noisy outcomes. The plan specifies fresh source identities, strict rates, repeated noise seeds, common block geometry, copy counts, a shared constraint gate and a clean-recovery stop condition. It is stored as experiments/block_matched_plan.json with the SHA256 below. This is a matched detector-allocation grid, not an optimized frontier against external storage systems. No physical claims or production changes follow from it.')
P.para(d,j['preregistered_plan_sha256'][:32]+'\n'+j['preregistered_plan_sha256'][32:])
P.h2(d,'Charge common padding rather than giving a short code free exposure')
P.para(d,'All candidate blocks are now 48 bases. The low-byte and full Fletcher words receive ten and four fixed zero trits after their original check region, respectively. The complete padded word is differentially mapped from its prescribed seed. Padding is ignored on decode, exposed to substitutions and charged to total encoded bases. The distance-five candidate uses all sixteen available check trits; the others allocate six or twelve trits to detection and leave the rest unused. They retain the same payload and outer parity architecture. This is deliberately not the best possible use of the low/full baseline budget: it isolates what detector allocation buys under one fixed framing design.')
P.para(d,'A mutation in ignored padding has no decoded payload effect, while the distance-five detector can reject mutations in its entire parity region. Ignored padding cannot be claimed as error protection. It does, however, preserve equal length and channel exposure, which the earlier unpadded comparison lacked. The first attempted clean run stopped before noisy outcomes because a reused helper checked the overwritten 48-base framing constant against an unpadded core. The audit corrected the helper to validate each original core length explicitly, then reran the unchanged clean gate and frozen plan. No scientific parameter or source-identity selection changed after seeing outcomes.')
P.h2(d,'A shared constraint gate and genuinely paired masks')
P.para(d,'Seed 930492 creates eight fresh 128-byte and eight fresh 2,048-byte identities. For each identity, all three clean candidates must have maximum whole-strand homopolymer at most two and whole-strand GC fraction from .45 through .55. If any candidate fails, all three versions of that identity are excluded; no replacement search is permitted. No identities failed this gate. The gate is only whole-strand HP2 and GC: it does not match HP1, sliding-window GC, forbidden motifs or a physical oligo pool. Reporting a gate without these definitions would hide a meaningful constraint difference.')
P.para(d,'For each copy count of one or three, all candidates expose the same 48-base block layout and total nucleotide budget. Equal strand length and RNG seeds now give identical Bernoulli changed-position masks and alternative-letter indices across candidates. Because their original sequences differ, the resulting replacement letters need not match. The ledger independently checks equal changed-base counts and total exposure for each paired message/copy/rate/repetition group. Every included file first passes exact clean decoding. Rates are zero, .005, .01, .02 and .03; each identity receives three repeats. The grid contains 1,440 file trials, not 1,440 independent files.')
P.table(d,'Matched three-copy 128-byte results: exact success / loud failure / silent wrong among 24 trials from eight identities.',['Strict p','Low-byte padded','Full Fletcher padded','Distance-five'],[['0','24 / 0 / 0','24 / 0 / 0','24 / 0 / 0'],['.005','24 / 0 / 0','24 / 0 / 0','24 / 0 / 0'],['.01','21 / 3 / 0','23 / 1 / 0','24 / 0 / 0'],['.02','15 / 8 / 1','22 / 2 / 0','22 / 2 / 0'],['.03','9 / 15 / 0','15 / 9 / 0','11 / 13 / 0']])
P.table(d,'Matched three-copy 2,048-byte results: exact success / loud failure / silent wrong among 24 trials from eight identities.',['Strict p','Low-byte padded','Full Fletcher padded','Distance-five'],[['0','24 / 0 / 0','24 / 0 / 0','24 / 0 / 0'],['.005','14 / 10 / 0','20 / 4 / 0','20 / 4 / 0'],['.01','7 / 17 / 0','13 / 11 / 0','12 / 12 / 0'],['.02','0 / 24 / 0','0 / 24 / 0','0 / 24 / 0'],['.03','0 / 24 / 0','0 / 24 / 0','0 / 24 / 0']])
P.h2(d,'The long-file negative and the small-file reversal')
P.para(d,'Stronger local detection improves several matched settings over the padded low-byte candidate, but distance-five does not uniformly dominate full Fletcher. At 128 bytes and .01 the exact counts are 24 versus 23; at .02 they tie at 22; at .03 full Fletcher recovers 15 while distance-five recovers 11. At 2,048 bytes and .01 full Fletcher recovers 13 while distance-five recovers 12. These are small finite differences with only eight source identities per size. The result does not justify a population ranking or an optimized parity choice. A code with a stronger low-order detection theorem can create more erasures than an incomplete detector at higher substitution multiplicity, without enough outer redundancy to repair them.')
P.para(d,'Every three-copy 2,048-byte trial fails loudly at strict .02 and .03 for all detectors. The one-parity-block architecture fails at that scale despite local detector redesign and copy redundancy. This is an important retained negative, not a failed test to discard. One-copy candidates pass clean recovery. The padded low-byte 128-byte condition at .005 has one exact success and 23 loud failures; every other one-copy noisy condition fails loudly in all 24 trials. Moving from one to three copies therefore buys observed recovery in some conditions at exactly three times the encoded nucleotide cost, while leaving the hardest long-file conditions unresolved.')
P.para(d,'One padded low-byte 128-byte trial at .02 returns a wrong file without an exception. Its exact reads and expected/actual bytes are saved and replay-tested. Neither stronger detector produces a silent wrong in this grid, but again finite absence is not a whole-file guarantee. The seeded unmodified-production counterexample from the earlier unpadded run remains separate. Padding changes framing, so this new padded wrong case is not attributed to the production decoder without its own valid replay route.')
P.h2(d,'A finite cost grid, not a solved frontier')
P.para(d,'At 128 bytes each clean strand is 1,248 nt; one and three copies cost 1,248 and 3,744 nt, giving .820513 and .273504 payload bits per encoded nt. At 2,048 bytes each strand is 18,528 nt; one and three copies cost 18,528 and 55,584 nt, giving .884283 and .294761 bits per encoded nt. All local check trits, ignored padding, finite headers, payload padding and the one outer parity block are charged. Primers, indexes, molecules, physical read depth and library dropout are not. Common within-grid budgets remove one confound without pretending these costs are complete laboratory costs.')
P.para(d,'A cost/recovery plot of this finite grid would retain both copy counts: one copy has lower cost and lower noisy recovery; three copies has higher cost and often higher recovery. Calling that plot an optimized frontier would be wrong because the grid does not vary outer parity, block size, code correction, oligo segmentation or screening overhead. No external baseline is included. The strongest defensible comparison is matched local detector allocation under HP2 and whole-strand GC constraints, with the observed full-Fletcher/distance-five reversals and universal long-file high-noise failures left visible.')
P.h2(d,'Reproduction and remaining work')
P.para(d,'Run python3 experiments/block_matched_geometry_audit.py. The frozen plan hash, geometry/gate record, exclusions, size-stratified summaries, all-trial ledger and saved wrong reads are retained. Tests check plan and ledger digests, no excluded identities, clean gates, exact geometry, paired masks/counts, wrong-output replay and isolation from the earlier unpadded benchmark module. The trial ledger digest is displayed below. Detector implementations stay audit-local, and no prior trials are overwritten.')
P.para(d,j['ledger_sha256'][:32]+'\n'+j['ledger_sha256'][32:])
P.para(d,'This completes a narrow matched geometry/constraint/copy comparison but does not complete the requested optimized frontier or establish biological storage. More outer redundancy, correction and segmented framing are candidate hypotheses, not validated next steps. Whole-paper source consistency, explanatory body growth, independent validation and the user-supplied verdict gate remain open. The additional manuscript pages report methods, costs, failed settings and scope; PDF totals still do not establish fifty audited full-text body pages.')
for t in d.tables[-2:]:
 for i,row in enumerate(t.rows):
  row._tr.get_or_add_trPr().append(OxmlElement('w:cantSplit'))
  for cell in row.cells:
   for para in cell.paragraphs:para.paragraph_format.keep_with_next=(i<len(t.rows)-1)
 Paragraph(t._tbl.getprevious(),d).paragraph_format.keep_with_next=True
P.save(d,str(p))
